1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,860
Right now, as you watch this, the Moon is
falling towards the Earth.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:08,160
So why doesn't it ever hit us?

3
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:10,900
And what does it have to do with an apple?

4
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:17,224
By the end of this film, you'll know how a
young man in a farmhouse garden, an

5
00:00:17,264 --> 00:00:23,467
imaginary cannon and one careful sum
showed that the pull that drops an apple

6
00:00:23,507 --> 00:00:25,980
also holds the Moon in the sky.

7
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:31,492
For thousands of years, people believed
the sky followed different rules from the

8
00:00:31,532 --> 00:00:31,900
Earth.

9
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:34,120
Down here, things fell.

10
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:39,200
A stone dropped, a cup tipped, a rider
fell from a horse.

11
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:45,987
But up there, the Moon, the Sun and the
planets moved in perfect circles, for

12
00:00:46,027 --> 00:00:48,320
ever, and never fell at all.

13
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,500
Why should a stone fall, but not the Moon?

14
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:55,140
It was one of the oldest mysteries in the
world.

15
00:00:55,980 --> 00:00:58,460
Picture yourself on a clear night in
sixteen sixty-five.

16
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:05,240
You would see the Moon rise over the
fields, huge and silent.

17
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:08,680
You would feel the cold ground under your
feet.

18
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:12,300
And if you dropped a stone, you would hear
it thud.

19
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:17,200
Would you ever think that the stone and
the Moon were doing the same thing?

20
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:19,200
One young man did.

21
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:23,460
And it started in a year when the whole
country was afraid.

22
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:30,220
In sixteen sixty-five, a terrible plague
swept through England.

23
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:34,680
To keep students safe, the university at
Cambridge closed its doors.

24
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:39,211
And a young student called Isaac Newton
went home, to his family's farmhouse in

25
00:01:39,251 --> 00:01:40,760
the village of Woolsthorpe.

26
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:45,200
He had been born in that same house, on
Christmas Day, sixteen forty-two.

27
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:49,520
His father had died a few months before.

28
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:55,685
Newton was so tiny, his mother later said,
that he could have fitted inside a quart

29
00:01:55,725 --> 00:01:56,525
pot.

30
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,680
For almost two years, he worked alone.

31
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:05,354
He studied light, invented new kinds of
mathematics, and asked questions no one

32
00:02:05,394 --> 00:02:06,520
had asked before.

33
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:12,453
Many years later, when he was an old man,
Newton told a friend how one of those

34
00:02:12,493 --> 00:02:13,440
questions began.

35
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:18,580
He was sitting in the garden, thinking,
when an apple fell from a tree.

36
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:21,060
The apple didn't hit him on the head.

37
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:22,680
That part was added later.

38
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:24,300
But it made him wonder.

39
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:28,860
The apple always falls straight down,
towards the centre of the Earth.

40
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:32,640
The pull that drags it down works at the
top of the tree.

41
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:36,800
It works at the top of a tall tower, and
at the top of a mountain.

42
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:39,320
So how far up does it reach?

43
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:41,840
Could it reach all the way to the Moon?

44
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,900
It was an extraordinary idea.

45
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:49,360
If the Earth was pulling on the Moon, then
the Moon should be falling.

46
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:52,620
But it was still up there, night after
night.

47
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:54,680
So what was holding it up?

48
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:01,580
Newton's answer was so simple it is easy
to miss.

49
00:03:02,420 --> 00:03:04,100
The Moon isn't held up at all.

50
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:05,860
It really is falling.

51
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:07,820
It just never lands.

52
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:14,378
To explain it, he imagined a cannon, on
top of a mountain so tall it poked above

53
00:03:14,418 --> 00:03:15,218
the air.

54
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:20,438
Fire a cannonball sideways, gently, and it
curves down and hits the ground near the

55
00:03:20,478 --> 00:03:21,780
bottom of the mountain.

56
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:26,160
Fire it faster, and it flies further
before it lands.

57
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:33,291
Now imagine firing it so fast that, as it
falls, the round Earth curves away beneath

58
00:03:33,331 --> 00:03:34,800
it, just as quickly.

59
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:36,640
What would you see?

60
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:41,060
The ball keeps falling, and keeps missing
the ground.

61
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:45,680
It falls all the way around the world, and
comes back to the mountain.

62
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:47,700
That is an orbit.

63
00:03:48,420 --> 00:03:53,732
Not something floating, but something
falling, and moving sideways so fast that

64
00:03:53,772 --> 00:03:54,720
it never hits.

65
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:57,260
And that's what the Moon is doing.

66
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:03,275
Every second, it moves sideways about a
kilometre, and falls a tiny bit towards

67
00:04:03,315 --> 00:04:04,115
the Earth.

68
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:07,560
Sideways and down, sideways and down, for
ever.

69
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:11,200
But an idea isn't proof.

70
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:16,611
If the same pull holds the Moon and drops
the apple, Newton should be able to

71
00:04:16,651 --> 00:04:18,380
calculate one from the other.

72
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:20,000
Could he?

73
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:26,220
Newton worked out that a pull like this
should get weaker with distance.

74
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:29,980
Twice as far away, it should be four times
weaker.

75
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:33,200
Three times as far, nine times weaker.

76
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:35,440
Why would it work like that?

77
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:37,700
Think of a can of spray paint.

78
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:42,260
Hold it close to a wall and you get a
small, dark spot.

79
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:49,377
Step back twice as far, and the same paint
spreads over a patch four times as big, so

80
00:04:49,417 --> 00:04:52,740
every part of it gets only a quarter as
much.

81
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:56,560
Newton's pull spreads out in the same way.

82
00:04:57,140 --> 00:05:01,600
The Moon is about sixty times further from
the centre of the Earth than you are.

83
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:05,840
So the pull on the Moon should be sixty
times sixty weaker.

84
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:09,740
Three thousand six hundred times weaker.

85
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:12,160
Then came the beautiful part.

86
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:16,840
In one second, an apple falls about five
metres.

87
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:23,658
If the pull on the Moon is 3,600 times
weaker, then in one minute, the Moon

88
00:05:23,698 --> 00:05:29,100
should fall about the same distance, five
metres, along its curve.

89
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:32,300
Newton checked it against the real Moon.

90
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:36,620
Years later, he wrote that the two
answered pretty nearly.

91
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:40,740
The apple and the Moon were obeying the
same rule.

92
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:46,280
But Newton was a careful, private man, and
his numbers weren't yet perfect.

93
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:50,020
He put the idea aside for almost twenty
years.

94
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:56,153
Then, in sixteen eighty-four, a young
astronomer called Edmond Halley came to

95
00:05:56,193 --> 00:05:57,620
see him in Cambridge.

96
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:00,080
Halley asked him a question.

97
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:06,039
If the Sun pulled on the planets with a
force that got weaker in just this way,

98
00:06:06,079 --> 00:06:07,900
what path would they follow?

99
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:10,720
An ellipse, Newton said at once.

100
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:12,860
He had worked it out years before.

101
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:17,580
Halley was amazed, and he begged Newton to
write it all down.

102
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:23,240
The result, three years later, was one of
the most important books ever written.

103
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:26,120
Newton called it the Principia.

104
00:06:26,940 --> 00:06:31,413
And because the Royal Society had spent
its money on a book about fish, Halley

105
00:06:31,453 --> 00:06:32,900
paid to print it himself.

106
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:37,880
Newton's law didn't stop at the Moon.

107
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:42,747
The same pull keeps the Earth falling
around the Sun, and the planets, and the

108
00:06:42,787 --> 00:06:43,760
moons of Jupiter.

109
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:49,180
The Moon even pulls back on the Earth, and
lifts the oceans into tides.

110
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:55,157
Halley used Newton's law to work out the
path of a bright comet, and he made a bold

111
00:06:55,197 --> 00:06:55,780
prediction.

112
00:06:55,860 --> 00:06:58,000
It would come back in seventeen fifty-
eight.

113
00:06:58,040 --> 00:07:02,720
Halley died years before it was due.

114
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:08,197
But on Christmas night, seventeen fifty-
eight, the comet appeared in the sky,

115
00:07:08,237 --> 00:07:09,220
right on time.

116
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:11,700
It has carried his name ever since.

117
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:14,900
And you can see Newton's cannon at work
today.

118
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:19,803
The International Space Station flies
around the Earth at about twenty-eight

119
00:07:19,843 --> 00:07:21,340
thousand kilometres an hour.

120
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:23,700
It's not floating above gravity.

121
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:27,700
It's falling, all the time, just like the
Moon.

122
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:33,980
The astronauts inside float because
they're falling too, right along with it.

123
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:38,900
Imagine being up there, with an apple
drifting in front of your face.

124
00:07:39,780 --> 00:07:41,780
It looks as if gravity has switched off.

125
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:43,360
But it hasn't.

126
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:49,520
You, the apple and the station are all
falling together, around the world.

127
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:52,260
Why was this so groundbreaking?

128
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:57,500
Because for the first time, the heavens
and the Earth followed the same rules.

129
00:07:58,340 --> 00:08:03,220
The force that drops a cup off your table
is the same force that steers the planets.

130
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:09,380
One law, written in mathematics, that
works everywhere we have ever looked.

131
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:14,880
So tonight, if you can see the Moon, take
a moment to look at it.

132
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:17,280
It is falling towards you, right now.

133
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:19,180
And it will keep on missing.

134
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:22,080
An apple, a cannon and a sum.

135
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:25,980
Together, they pulled the heavens down to
Earth.

136
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:29,020
But gravity can bring more than apples
down.

137
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:34,540
Sixty-six million years ago, something far
bigger fell out of the sky.

138
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:38,220
And the clue was hidden in a thin layer of
clay.
